<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmed Urabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1841">1841</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ahmed Orabi, Urabi Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmed Urabi was an Egyptian army officer who led a nationalist movement against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive</a> Tewfik and European control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s finances. Of peasant origin, he opposed the dominance of Turco-Circassian officers and became minister of war in 1882. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> invaded, defeated his army at Tel el-Kebir in September 1882 and occupied Egypt, and Urabi was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> until 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-urabi.jpg" alt="Ahmed Urabi" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arabi_(1906)_-_TIMEA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
